  "transcript": {
    "keith-crossland-dustin-moss-renewal-edmonton.mp4": "Two Edmontan neighbors share a wall, and their mortgages renew the same month. But in five years, one of them pays thousands more in interest. The left side waits for the bank's letter, which can come just 21 days out. So they sign off on the first offer. The right side starts four months early, renewing in March, day 120 is about now. They get other lenders to hold a rate, then compare. Say the first offer is half a point higher. That's about $80 a month. Over five years, what's your guess? About $7,000 more in interest. Keith Crossland and Dustin Moss spent years inside big banks. They treat the first offer as a starting point. Send this to the neighbor renewing next."
  },
